Equivalence Groups Found
└── Modbus TCP client for reading/writing holding registers
    ├── Candidates : pymodbus, pyModbusTCP, modbus-tk, minimalmodbus
    ├── Used APIs  : ModbusTcpClient, client.connect, read_holding_registers, write_register, rr.isError, rr.registers
    ├── Pitfalls   : minimalmodbus's API returns/raises differently (no isError() result wrapper) and is primarily built for serial devices, requiring a 
    │   serial-to-TCP bridge or adapter for full compatibility; pyModbusTCP uses read_holding_registers()/write_single_register() naming and returns plain 
    │   lists/booleans instead of a response object with isError(), requiring call-site changes; modbus-tk requires explicit master/slave configuration and 
    │   uses a different execute() based API rather than direct read/write methods; Unit/slave ID parameter naming and defaults differ across libraries (unit= 
    │   vs slave= vs contained in instrument config); Connection/retry/timeout semantics differ significantly between libraries
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── All four packages implement Modbus master/client functionality capable of reading and writing holding registers over TCP or serial. pymodbus and 
        pyModbusTCP are TCP-native and closest in API shape (client object with connect/read/write methods). modbus-tk also supports TCP master mode with a 
        similar register read/write model. minimalmodbus is primarily designed for serial/RTU communication and uses a very different function-based API 
        (read_register/write_register on an Instrument object) and different error handling (exceptions instead of an isError() result object), making it a 
        viable functional substitute only with significant glue code, as the source comment itself acknowledges.

